WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.627 --> 00:00:04.963
Airbnb is huge today.

00:00:04.963 --> 00:00:07.382
On any given night you
could have as many as

00:00:07.382 --> 00:00:11.553
2 million people staying in an
Airbnb all around the world.

00:00:11.553 --> 00:00:14.639
You know, our mission is that everyone 
can belong anywhere.

00:00:14.639 --> 00:00:15.682
We're talking, you know,

00:00:15.682 --> 00:00:19.019
300 million stays on any average year

00:00:19.019 --> 00:00:21.354
and there's tyranny in numbers.

00:00:22.647 --> 00:00:26.026
The vast majority of
Airbnb stays don't result

00:00:26.026 --> 00:00:29.988
in a safety incident, but
sometimes things go wrong.

00:00:29.988 --> 00:00:33.158
And for Airbnb, you know,
we're talking thousands

00:00:33.158 --> 00:00:35.869
of safety incidents that occur every year

00:00:35.869 --> 00:00:39.080
that the company has to
handle and has to respond to.

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:44.044
I'm talking situations like
kidnappings, hostage cases,

00:00:44.044 --> 00:00:46.880
we're talking murder, we're talking rape,

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:49.007
we're talking, you know,
the worst violent crimes

00:00:49.007 --> 00:00:54.012
that occur in our society
happen inside Airbnbs.

00:00:54.095 --> 00:00:56.931
Airbnb never wants these kinds of cases

00:00:56.931 --> 00:00:58.683
to end up in the headlines.

00:00:58.683 --> 00:01:01.102
You know, that's really
bad for their brand,

00:01:01.102 --> 00:01:03.313
their reputation, and their public image,

00:01:03.313 --> 00:01:05.315
particularly because this is a business

00:01:05.315 --> 00:01:08.109
that's based on strangers
trusting one another.

00:01:08.109 --> 00:01:11.446
So when crimes like this occur,

00:01:11.446 --> 00:01:13.615
that kind of threatens
their entire business model.

00:01:25.668 --> 00:01:27.295
I'm Olivia Carville, I'm a reporter

00:01:27.295 --> 00:01:29.798
on Bloomberg's Technology
team and I've been covering

00:01:29.798 --> 00:01:33.968
online travel companies like
Airbnb for the last two years.

00:01:37.889 --> 00:01:41.351
Airbnb is a startup that's
based in Silicon Valley

00:01:41.351 --> 00:01:43.728
and like many of the
sharing economy startups

00:01:43.728 --> 00:01:46.356
it managed to grow so big so fast

00:01:46.356 --> 00:01:49.192
by essentially skirting regulations.

00:01:53.238 --> 00:01:56.241
It was based on that premise
of move fast and break things

00:01:56.241 --> 00:01:59.160
and it wanted to work against regulations

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:02.747
in order to grow as big as
possible and win the popular vote

00:02:02.747 --> 00:02:04.833
because it became so popular so fast

00:02:04.833 --> 00:02:07.585
that cities could no longer control it.

00:02:07.585 --> 00:02:11.714
On a peak night this past
August, we had 1 million people

00:02:11.714 --> 00:02:15.009
from around the world
coming from 180 countries

00:02:15.009 --> 00:02:17.846
in a single night,
staying in an Airbnb home.

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:22.934
The vast majority of Airbnb
stays, we're talking, you know

00:02:22.934 --> 00:02:26.521
2 million people staying in
an Airbnb on any given night,

00:02:26.521 --> 00:02:28.523
don't result in a safety incident

00:02:28.523 --> 00:02:30.859
but sometimes things go wrong.

00:02:33.236 --> 00:02:36.739
And for the company, you know, it was,

00:02:36.739 --> 00:02:39.576
dealing with these cases
was a really challenging,

00:02:39.576 --> 00:02:41.995
difficult, complicated area.

00:02:41.995 --> 00:02:44.455
You had hosts and guests and sometimes

00:02:44.455 --> 00:02:46.332
they disagreed on what happened.

00:02:46.332 --> 00:02:49.627
You had regulatory issues in
some of their biggest cities.

00:02:49.627 --> 00:02:52.755
Then you also had, you know,
the growth of the platform

00:02:52.755 --> 00:02:54.799
and the fact that if you're
going to try and prevent

00:02:54.799 --> 00:02:57.093
some of these incidents,
it could cut into revenue

00:02:57.093 --> 00:03:00.054
and cut into the number
of listings that you have.

00:03:00.054 --> 00:03:03.183
So for Airbnb, it was
about trying to figure out

00:03:03.183 --> 00:03:07.145
the best way to handle these
crises in a way that's going to

00:03:07.145 --> 00:03:09.439
not only protect the individual in crisis,

00:03:09.439 --> 00:03:13.651
but also protect the company's
public image, its brand,

00:03:13.651 --> 00:03:16.654
its reputation, which is
essentially what its business

00:03:16.654 --> 00:03:17.614
is based upon.

00:03:20.116 --> 00:03:22.327
As Airbnb grew, like, way too big for them

00:03:22.327 --> 00:03:24.996
to be able to take all these calls alone,

00:03:24.996 --> 00:03:28.458
they developed a team of a
hundred highly specialized agents

00:03:28.458 --> 00:03:31.377
that handle the worst of the worst cases.

00:03:31.377 --> 00:03:34.047
Over the course of my reporting,
I've talked to almost 50

00:03:34.047 --> 00:03:37.926
former employees at Airbnb
and you know, seven or eight

00:03:37.926 --> 00:03:40.803
of those work directly on this elite unit.

00:03:40.803 --> 00:03:43.056
These safety agents, many of them come

00:03:43.056 --> 00:03:46.184
from military backgrounds
or trauma backgrounds.

00:03:46.184 --> 00:03:49.229
They're trained to handle
traumatic situations

00:03:49.229 --> 00:03:52.065
and to try and, you know, help
people who are going through

00:03:52.065 --> 00:03:55.568
what, what may be, you know,
the worst day in their lives.

00:03:55.568 --> 00:03:57.737
For these safety agents,
a lot of them understood

00:03:57.737 --> 00:03:59.280
that they had a dual role.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:02.075
First and foremost, they wanted to protect

00:04:02.075 --> 00:04:05.286
the user in crisis but they also knew

00:04:05.286 --> 00:04:07.497
they had a responsibility to protect

00:04:07.497 --> 00:04:09.707
the company's brand and public image.

00:04:10.583 --> 00:04:12.627
I mean, everything about
housing in New York

00:04:12.627 --> 00:04:14.545
is a total mess and it's always chaotic.

00:04:14.545 --> 00:04:17.882
It's not too surprising
that Airbnb's biggest battle

00:04:17.882 --> 00:04:19.217
has broken out here.

00:04:19.217 --> 00:04:23.346
2015 was a really
challenging year for Airbnb.

00:04:23.346 --> 00:04:26.599
Airbnb's listings between 2010 and 2014

00:04:26.599 --> 00:04:28.977
were essentially illegal hotels.

00:04:28.977 --> 00:04:32.188
You know, the company was seven years old now,

00:04:32.188 --> 00:04:36.276
it had grown huge, it
was now a household name.

00:04:36.276 --> 00:04:41.155
But by growing that big that
fast they also came up against,

00:04:41.155 --> 00:04:43.700
you know, a pushback in regulations.

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:46.160
Now, Airbnb's under attack
in a lot of major cities.

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:47.245
Yeah, so what are they saying about this?

00:04:47.245 --> 00:04:48.788
I mean -
They are, well, they can't

00:04:48.788 --> 00:04:50.707
give up on New York, 'cause
New York's too important

00:04:50.707 --> 00:04:52.875
for every kind of reason.

00:04:52.875 --> 00:04:55.837
They knew that they couldn't
lose the battle in New York

00:04:55.837 --> 00:04:58.006
because it was one of
their biggest markets

00:04:58.006 --> 00:05:01.009
and also it could result
in a domino effect.

00:05:01.009 --> 00:05:04.053
If other cities around
America saw New York City

00:05:04.053 --> 00:05:06.681
either backing down on regulations

00:05:06.681 --> 00:05:10.059
or enforcing stricter
regulations that could in turn

00:05:10.059 --> 00:05:12.228
be rolled out right across the country.

00:05:12.228 --> 00:05:14.772
I think it's gonna be awkward
when they have to, you know

00:05:14.772 --> 00:05:17.400
putting their prospectus,
their risk factors

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:19.277
when they have to say,
well, you know, one risk

00:05:19.277 --> 00:05:21.112
is that the whole thing
might be somewhat illegal

00:05:21.112 --> 00:05:22.947
in our biggest market.

00:05:23.906 --> 00:05:27.493
And in December of that year,
a young woman from Australia

00:05:27.493 --> 00:05:29.954
flew into New York and stayed at an Airbnb

00:05:29.954 --> 00:05:31.622
with a group of her friends.

00:05:39.422 --> 00:05:42.425
They picked up a key from
the local corner bodega

00:05:42.425 --> 00:05:45.261
without even needing identification.

00:05:45.261 --> 00:05:48.056
The police report said
this 29 year old woman

00:05:48.056 --> 00:05:50.141
left her friends at a Midtown bar

00:05:50.141 --> 00:05:52.310
and returned to the Airbnb on her own.

00:05:53.227 --> 00:05:56.522
She thought that she was
alone, but she wasn't.

00:05:56.522 --> 00:05:58.441
There was a man inside the apartment

00:05:58.441 --> 00:06:01.527
hiding in the shadows
with a kitchen knife.

00:06:03.738 --> 00:06:08.618
He grabbed her, dragged her
into a room and raped her.

00:06:08.618 --> 00:06:09.452
He later fled.

00:06:12.538 --> 00:06:16.125
About an hour after
this attack he came back

00:06:16.125 --> 00:06:18.669
and police were in the
apartment at the time.

00:06:18.669 --> 00:06:23.049
They opened his backpack and
found three items of interest,

00:06:23.049 --> 00:06:27.345
the kitchen knife, one
of the woman's earrings,

00:06:27.345 --> 00:06:30.223
and a set of keys to the Airbnb.

00:06:33.142 --> 00:06:35.520
This case was a really
complicated one for Airbnb,

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:38.815
partly because it
occurred in New York City

00:06:38.815 --> 00:06:41.401
where they were fighting
this really tough battle

00:06:41.401 --> 00:06:43.653
with city officials over regulations.

00:06:43.653 --> 00:06:47.573
And partly because the
alleged rapist had the keys

00:06:47.573 --> 00:06:49.867
and he was inside the apartment.

00:06:49.867 --> 00:06:53.287
That meant that Airbnb
needed to do what it could

00:06:53.287 --> 00:06:55.081
to protect itself.

00:06:55.081 --> 00:06:58.543
They wanted to find out, you
know, how did he get the keys?

00:06:58.543 --> 00:07:00.128
That was a really big question for them.

00:07:00.128 --> 00:07:02.046
They launched an investigation
to find that out,

00:07:02.046 --> 00:07:04.549
but before they did anything like that,

00:07:04.549 --> 00:07:06.134
before they thought to kind of protect

00:07:06.134 --> 00:07:08.886
their own reputation
here they really focused

00:07:08.886 --> 00:07:11.889
on doing what they could
to help this young woman.

00:07:11.889 --> 00:07:14.892
They paid for her to move to a hotel.

00:07:14.892 --> 00:07:17.728
They paid for her mother
to fly in from Australia.

00:07:17.728 --> 00:07:20.022
They paid for both of them to fly home.

00:07:20.022 --> 00:07:23.985
They offered to cover any
health or counselling costs

00:07:23.985 --> 00:07:25.945
that she might need later.

00:07:25.945 --> 00:07:28.114
But inside the company internally,

00:07:28.114 --> 00:07:30.199
they also launched an investigation.

00:07:30.199 --> 00:07:35.037
They wanted to know how did the
alleged rapist get the keys?

00:07:35.037 --> 00:07:37.206
How did he get inside that Airbnb?

00:07:37.206 --> 00:07:40.293
And when they found out
that the keys had been left

00:07:40.293 --> 00:07:43.379
at the corner bodega, that
then created, you know,

00:07:43.379 --> 00:07:45.715
a problem of liability for Airbnb.

00:07:45.715 --> 00:07:48.176
They didn't have a policy
around key exchange.

00:07:48.176 --> 00:07:50.178
They didn't tell their hosts how they did

00:07:50.178 --> 00:07:53.055
or didn't have to hand keys over to guests

00:07:53.055 --> 00:07:54.599
as they were arriving.

00:07:54.599 --> 00:07:57.018
So for Airbnb, they also, you know,

00:07:57.018 --> 00:07:59.604
they wanted to know what was
going to be said in court.

00:07:59.604 --> 00:08:01.898
They wanted to know if any
media were going to pick up

00:08:01.898 --> 00:08:04.775
on the story and they wanted to know

00:08:04.775 --> 00:08:07.236
what their legal liability
potentially could be.

00:08:13.326 --> 00:08:16.078
A week later, the alleged
rapist was indicted

00:08:16.078 --> 00:08:18.498
in the Supreme Court in New York

00:08:18.498 --> 00:08:21.292
and Airbnb sent a staff member to find out

00:08:21.292 --> 00:08:22.293
if the company was going to be mentioned

00:08:22.293 --> 00:08:24.712
in any of the court records.

00:08:24.712 --> 00:08:27.632
And it wasn't, it wasn't
mentioned in the police report.

00:08:27.632 --> 00:08:29.550
It wasn't mentioned by prosecutors.

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:32.678
There's no mention of Airbnb
really in any public record.

00:08:32.678 --> 00:08:35.181
This case has never
been reported until now.

00:08:35.181 --> 00:08:39.352
And you know, for Airbnb,
that was kind of lucky,

00:08:39.352 --> 00:08:41.854
this case never landed in the media.

00:08:41.854 --> 00:08:45.733
And you know, 18 months later
when this woman's lawyer

00:08:45.733 --> 00:08:47.818
reached out to the company,

00:08:47.818 --> 00:08:50.530
they signed a settlement for $7 million

00:08:50.530 --> 00:08:53.616
and part of that settlement,
there's a clause that says

00:08:53.616 --> 00:08:58.371
she cannot assert or imply responsibility

00:08:58.371 --> 00:08:59.747
on the part of the company.

00:09:00.873 --> 00:09:03.376
We've spoken to legal experts who indicate

00:09:03.376 --> 00:09:07.046
that what this really did
is gag the woman from saying

00:09:07.046 --> 00:09:08.506
"I blame Airbnb".

00:09:29.819 --> 00:09:32.363
Airbnb is one of the few
companies in the world

00:09:32.363 --> 00:09:38.077
that has this like online-offline
kind of hybrid situation

00:09:38.077 --> 00:09:41.747
where you have users who meet online

00:09:41.747 --> 00:09:45.793
and then actually meet
in the offline world

00:09:45.793 --> 00:09:47.670
and stay under the same roof.

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:50.840
And that means that the
company at the end of the day

00:09:50.840 --> 00:09:53.551
really has to take more responsibility

00:09:53.551 --> 00:09:56.262
than other social media
platforms might do.

00:09:56.262 --> 00:09:58.347
With Airbnb's terms of service,

00:09:58.347 --> 00:10:00.891
that really means that
whenever there's a dispute

00:10:00.891 --> 00:10:03.519
between hosts and guests and the company,

00:10:03.519 --> 00:10:06.939
this is all handled behind
closed doors and arbitration.

00:10:06.939 --> 00:10:09.317
Most of these cases don't
even make it to the courtroom

00:10:09.317 --> 00:10:12.153
and when they do the
company will settle them

00:10:12.153 --> 00:10:14.905
so it doesn't go on through the courts.

00:10:14.905 --> 00:10:18.868
And what that means is that
there hasn't really been

00:10:18.868 --> 00:10:21.078
a legal precedent that's set

00:10:21.078 --> 00:10:24.332
to determine what Airbnb's
liability is here.

00:10:24.332 --> 00:10:26.709
You know, is it really responsible
for when things go wrong

00:10:26.709 --> 00:10:28.294
inside its listings?

00:10:50.441 --> 00:10:53.027
What I learned over the
course of my reporting

00:10:53.027 --> 00:10:55.196
is that this is anything but simple.

00:10:55.196 --> 00:10:58.366
This is a complicated, nuanced area.

00:10:58.366 --> 00:11:00.534
There are difficult trade-offs here.

00:11:00.534 --> 00:11:03.996
The company also has
to have hard, you know,

00:11:03.996 --> 00:11:06.540
tough conversations about policy.

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:10.044
Airbnb's business model
is based on strangers

00:11:10.044 --> 00:11:11.462
trusting one another.

00:11:11.462 --> 00:11:14.256
We often hear from the founders
and the chief executive

00:11:14.256 --> 00:11:17.635
himself saying trust is
their number one priority.

00:11:17.635 --> 00:11:19.512
I've actually heard Brian
Chesky, the chief executive

00:11:19.512 --> 00:11:24.016
of Airbnb talking about how
he didn't build a website

00:11:24.016 --> 00:11:26.602
or a platform that allows people to stay

00:11:26.602 --> 00:11:28.020
in each other's homes,

00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:31.107
what he built was a community
that trusts one another.

00:11:31.107 --> 00:11:34.610
And when that premise
has undercut Airbnb's

00:11:34.610 --> 00:11:36.654
entire business model is under threat.

